    "note": "Both lines are approximations, not official index series. Each region's market cap at a past month is its Q2-2026 Dimensional baseline (Russell 3000 / MSCI World ex USA IMI / MSCI EM IMI, as in the donut above) scaled backward by a price index, without dividends (paid-out cash leaves market cap). Solid line: all three regions, via SPY / EFA / EEM, from 2003 (EEM inception); EFA excludes Canada and is large/mid cap only. Dashed line: US vs developed markets only (no emerging markets), S&P 500 vs MSCI World price index (USD) with Intl Developed as the residual, from 1972 (the oldest MSCI World history on Yahoo Finance). Emerging markets cannot be included before 2003 with free data, so the dashed line sits above the solid one by roughly the EM share. Share counts are assumed constant: net issuance outside the US and buybacks inside it are ignored, which very likely understates the US share in the early decades — treat pre-1990 values as indicative only. Japan: Nikkei 225 (price-weighted, 225 stocks, not cap-weighted like TOPIX, which Yahoo does not carry) converted to USD with the FRED yen rate and anchored on Japan's share of International Developed in today's MSCI ACWI weights; it tracks Japan's true cap only roughly."
