    "note": "FCF yield: free cash flow of the whole US corporate sector (listed and unlisted) over its enterprise value, rebuilt from BEA NIPA table 1.14 and Fed Z.1 (via FRED) with the method of Atkeson, Heathcote & Perri (Minneapolis Fed Staff Report 682, 2026): FCF = gross value added − compensation − production taxes − business transfers − corporate income tax − gross investment; quarterly, not smoothed. It matches the paper's published averages (1980–81: 3.1 %, 2021–22: 3.1–3.2 %, mean ~3.2 %) but sits above its latest figure (2.59 % at Q1 2026, shown as a dot), most likely because the paper's FDI/residence adjustment to enterprise value is not replicated. Treat the last two years of the rebuilt line as indicative. Earnings yield = 1 / Shiller CAPE (10-year average of real earnings, so a real yield); 10Y = Shiller long rate before 1953, then the 10-year Treasury (nominal). Sources: FRED, multpl.com (Shiller data)."
