    "note": "Expected return = Gordon growth model, E[R] = yield × (1 + g) + g, using only data known at each date. Dividend version: S&P 500 trailing dividend yield + trailing 10-year growth of the nominal dividend (Shiller data via multpl.com). FCF version: US corporate-sector FCF yield (card above) + trailing 10-year growth of nominal corporate gross value added (BEA, via FRED). Dashed FCF line: same yield with a smoothed g = trailing 20-year real growth of corporate value added + expected inflation (Cleveland Fed 10-year, from 1982; trailing 10-year GDP-deflator inflation before), which strips out inflation bursts and slumps from the trailing nominal g. Purple line: smoothed-g expected return plus the effect of the FCF yield (4-quarter average) moving back to its own average since 1952 over 10 years, using only the history known at each date — the valuation-reversion term consistent with the FCF reading of the yields card. Right-hand scale, today only: the same with the yield published by Atkeson-Heathcote-Perri (2.59 % → their 3.18 % mean), and, for contrast, a CAPE falling from its level to 30 or 25. Realized = S&P 500 nominal total return over the NEXT 1, 5 or 10 years (dividends reinvested), annualized and plotted at the start date, so the line stops h years before today. The gap between realized and expected is mostly the change in valuation multiples, which Gordon assumes away: large over 1 year, much smaller over 10. Since the 1980s buybacks have replaced part of dividends, so the dividend version understates expected returns. Overlapping windows: since 1881 there are only ~14 independent 10-year periods, so R² values are indicative. Nominal, in USD; the FCF yield covers all US corporations, the realized return the S&P 500 only."
